{"w":2400,"h":3395,"lines":[{"w":0.061893049875895195,"h":0.017734702821333936,"t":"Test 4","y":0.10014427646350388,"c":1,"x":0.12748968894744081},{"w":0.12769060770670571,"h":0.017879983702031588,"c":1,"x":0.45673082773090701,"t":"READING","y":0.14222279883197619},{"t":"READING PASSAGE 1","h":0.020437861780676525,"c":1,"x":0.129629633522266,"w":0.29218106587727866,"y":0.19758539407078946},{"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 1-13, which are based on Reading","w":0.76543207804361979,"h":0.020386501532420942,"x":0.12962964004344579,"y":0.23255813934475489,"c":1},{"t":"Passage 1 below.","h":0.017441860778592888,"c":1,"y":0.25290697657597283,"w":0.16255143483479817,"x":0.127572019103881},{"c":1,"t":"The return of the huarango","y":0.28474676047820946,"w":0.38477366129557294,"h":0.022084805653710182,"x":0.33127572163028524},{"h":0.024757694159014632,"y":0.31806434230613279,"t":"The arid valleys of southern Peru are welcoming","w":0.51074523925781246,"c":1,"x":0.2693032452716006},{"w":0.28395062764485685,"h":0.017470964694612956,"c":1,"y":0.33863368661373539,"t":"the return of a native plant","x":0.38271604417588329},{"y":0.37120046454658451,"x":0.12942715207845074,"h":0.023692286056680389,"c":1,"t":"The south coast of Peru is a narrow, 2,000-kilometre-long strip of desert squeezed between the","w":0.75969075520833318},{"x":0.12944094538252196,"y":0.38836250302917119,"w":0.78431915283203124,"h":0.022087209497099081,"t":"Andes and the Pacific Ocean. It is also one of the most fragile ecosystems on Earth. It hardly ever","c":1},{"h":0.023141020740019336,"y":0.40498454661929717,"c":1,"w":0.76785898844401046,"t":"rains there, and the only year-round source of water is located tens of metres below the surface.","x":0.12737725382172801},{"t":"This is why the huarango tree is so suited to life there: it has the longest roots of any tree in the","h":0.0208410817967426,"c":1,"w":0.76367411295572918,"y":0.42219185589033947,"x":0.12948225600781843},{"h":0.022248415278602107,"w":0.78227228800455728,"y":0.43884019305165789,"t":"world. They stretch down 50-80 metres and, as well as sucking up water for the tree, they bring it","x":0.12946157215314144,"c":1},{"x":0.12947660692170515,"w":0.53524480183919265,"h":0.020215917391827598,"c":1,"y":0.45763312578874138,"t":"into the higher subsoil, creating a water source for other plant life."},{"y":0.4882280950387663,"h":0.022556024108814854,"t":"Dr David Beresford-Jones, archaeobotanist at Cambridge University, has been studying the role","c":1,"w":0.7678597005208333,"x":0.13150589598638759},{"y":0.50552009188244951,"c":1,"t":"of the huarango tree in landscape change in the Lower Ica Valley in southern Peru. He believes","h":0.021371295511090738,"w":0.75958897908528633,"x":0.13152511274735587},{"c":1,"x":0.13151213303569004,"y":0.52157414060447882,"w":0.77602208455403643,"t":"the huarango was key to the ancient people's diet and, because it could reach deep water sources,","h":0.021845897319600516},{"t":"it allowed local people to withstand years of drought when their other crops failed. But over the","x":0.1294612005770322,"w":0.76784764607747391,"c":1,"y":0.53833814910988809,"h":0.022098233477948481},{"y":0.55480531956992951,"h":0.022468528702626944,"t":"centuries huarango trees were gradually replaced with crops. Cutting down native woodland leads","w":0.78439824422200521,"c":1,"x":0.13147397135340771},{"y":0.57209766316555311,"c":1,"x":0.12949583116933999,"t":"to erosion, as there is nothing to keep the soil in place. So when the huarangos go, the land turns","h":0.021579926651414394,"w":0.77600433349609377},{"c":1,"w":0.51052820841471358,"h":0.019005704684308067,"t":"into a desert. Nothing grows at all in the Lower Ica Valley now.","x":0.12951237207907956,"y":0.59104857023797852},{"c":1,"x":0.13152082013570152,"t":"For centuries the huarango tree was vital to the people of the neighbouring Middle Ica Valley","w":0.74728805541992172,"y":0.62166307568149803,"h":0.022385811777642872},{"y":0.63840313849449548,"w":0.77609547932942713,"h":0.022183978796286086,"c":1,"x":0.13149643169950878,"t":"too. They grew vegetables under it and ate products made from its seed pods. Its leaves and bark"},{"t":"were used for herbal remedies, while its branches were used for charcoal for cooking and heating,","c":1,"w":0.78633188883463534,"y":0.65461188241566759,"x":0.13355743189414065,"h":0.023130651637999544},{"y":0.67277145429178487,"c":1,"x":0.13361179747169491,"t":"and its trunk was used to build houses. But now it is disappearing rapidly. The majority of the","w":0.75127792358398426,"h":0.01893391738370398},{"t":"huarango forests in the valley have already been cleared for fuel and agriculture - initially, these","x":0.13357765288322318,"y":0.68837761142311926,"w":0.77194117228190096,"h":0.021644619244147512,"c":1},{"t":"were smallholdings, but now they're huge farms producing crops for the international market.","c":1,"y":0.70518118685140496,"x":0.13355663775682633,"h":0.022914830310044154,"w":0.75342386881510415},{"x":0.13360007443762356,"h":0.024225157478251713,"y":0.73714338583072025,"t":"'Of the forests that were here 1,000 years ago, 99 per cent have already gone,' says botanist Oliver","c":1,"w":0.78841700236002599},{"x":0.13557986405149752,"w":0.75759455362955719,"y":0.75403799135576133,"h":0.023876797886141943,"c":1,"t":"Whaley from Kew Gardens in London, who, together with ethnobotanist Dr William Milliken,"},{"w":0.75753896077473959,"h":0.022051869629409415,"c":1,"t":"is running a pioneering project to protect and restore the rapidly disappearing habitat. In order","x":0.13357212862413698,"y":0.77137853712807469},{"c":1,"t":"to succeed, Whaley needs to get the local people on board, and that has meant overcoming local","h":0.021808696158221452,"w":0.76988637288411454,"x":0.13358882802317842,"y":0.7887455630986433},{"w":0.78228281656901044,"y":0.80466479997906148,"x":0.13354572544910204,"c":1,"h":0.022347653572073578,"t":"prejudices. 'Increasingly aspirational communities think that if you plant food trees in your home"},{"w":0.75763422648111978,"h":0.024381637573242188,"y":0.82079594794290689,"c":1,"x":0.135575414866629,"t":"or street, it shows you are poor, and still need to grow your own food,' he says. In order to stop"},{"x":0.13360479848939216,"t":"the Middle Ica Valley going the same way as the Lower Ica Valley, Whaley is encouraging locals","y":0.83818053702772866,"w":0.77602727254231763,"c":1,"h":0.02215753651900898},{"h":0.021945869122292905,"c":1,"t":"to love the huarangos again. 'It's a process of cultural resuscitation,' he says. He has already set","x":0.13359449198619511,"y":0.85475956328922165,"w":0.76779464721679691},{"c":1,"h":0.023107016465689134,"y":0.87159499588446432,"t":"up a huarango festival to reinstate a sense of pride in their eco-heritage, and has helped local","x":0.1355997771572432,"w":0.74326156616210937},{"h":0.019200726027202353,"x":0.1356025759238943,"c":1,"y":0.89173224931057793,"w":0.3233818308512369,"t":"schoolchildren plant thousands of trees."},{"x":0.13580246864979811,"t":"80","w":0.026748971939086902,"c":1,"y":0.92005813935192304,"h":0.013081395583944722}]}